# Athea shell integration.
#
# This file is handed to mksh through the ENV variable, so only the
# interactive shells spawned by Athea read it. It installs shell
# integration marks (OSC 133, the FinalTerm convention used by VS Code,
# iTerm2 and others) around the prompt:
#
#   D;<code>  - the previous command finished with this exit code
#   A         - prompt starts
#   B         - prompt ends (input region starts)
#
# The marks are invisible to the user; the Athea stream parser uses them
# to locate real command boundaries. The prompt itself is kept empty:
# the app renders its own input surface, so a visible prompt would only
# pollute the transcript.

case $- in *i*) ;; *) return ;; esac

PS1=$'\E]133;D;'"$?"$'\a\E]133;A\a\E]133;B\a'
